\subsection{Recognizing Sensitive Attributes}\label{sect:ep}
The simplest way for a model to remove its bias is to be entirely blind or unaware of the sensitive attribute. For contrastive models, such outcome is undesirable since it impacts utility; e.g. the model may not distinguish between ``father'' or ``mother'' in the caption. To examine if the improvement in RB and AB due to data balancing impacts the ability of the model to recognize those attributes, we use the zero-shot classification setting with the two labels ``man'' and ``woman'' for each image in  FairFace, UTKFace and MIAP. When aggregating errors using  Wilcoxon's signed rank test~\citep{wilcoxon1992individual} with the null hypothesis that (\verb|baseline|, \verb|balanced|, \verb|proxies|) yield the same performance, we have $p>0.05$, indicating the absence of statistically significant differences.
